2186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348033 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348033 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186345380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348033 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f399272f-f649-5a0c-8d81-b24dff62d20e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348268 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348268 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348268 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348268 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the differential $\\text{d}f$ for the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348268 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a08547f7-2388-51c6-b26c-7a79df9eb29f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348268 #edb/ref 1074 "We compute the differential using the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\,\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}+\\frac{3{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z}{{x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79746" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1071 "Given a rectangular solid, find the differential $\\text{d}V$ for the volume $V$ of the rectangular solid if the lengths of the sides are $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "eb226681-0351-549f-b81b-c5875e8a6018" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348270 #edb/ref 1074 "The volume of the rectangular solid is $V=abc.$ Let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(a,b,c) & =(\\frac{∂V}{∂a})\\,\\text{d}a+(\\frac{∂V}{∂b})\\,\\text{d}b+(\\frac{∂V}{∂c})\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =bc\\,\\text{d}a+ac\\,\\text{d}b+ab\\,\\text{d}c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=1,$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}V(2,5,1) & =5\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}a+2\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}b+5\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}c \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{d}a+2\\,\\text{d}b+10\\,\\text{d}c.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79815" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1071 "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z,$ use the differential $\\text{d}f$ to estimate the change $Δf$ in the value of $f(x,y,z)$ from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a50b343-0303-5110-8cf9-6975a85c04cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348293 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y,z) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y+(\\frac{∂f}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+(x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,\\text{d}y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)\\,\\text{d}x+(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\,\\text{d}y-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+1\\,\\text{d}y+0\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,0+Δy,\\frac{\\pi }{2}+Δz),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz.\n$$\n\nMoving from $(1,0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ to $(0,1,1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =0-1=-1, \\\\ Δy & =1-0=1 \\\\ Δz & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\,Δx+1\\,Δy+0\\,Δz \\\\  & ≈0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (1)+0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ≈1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(x,y,z)$ increases by approximately $1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348300 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348300 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79836" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348300 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348300 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348300 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e921641-2f51-5af1-85b0-a27e011ac9c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348300 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡0\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =0\\,\\text{d}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+Δx,0+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈0\\,Δx+\\frac{1}{2}\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12)=f(1,0.12),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1,0.12).$\n\nMoving from $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)$ to $(1,0.12),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1-\\frac{\\pi }{3}, \\\\ Δy & =0.12-0=0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈0\\cdot (1-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0.12) \\\\  & ≈0+0.5\\cdot 0.12 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.06.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0.12) & ≈f(\\frac{\\pi }{3},0)+0.06 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.12) & ≈\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0+0.06 \\\\  & ≈0.06.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348301 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186348033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348301 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348301 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348301 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the value of ${e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}$ at the point $(9,3).$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348301 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c9a3a87-a14e-5269-82cd-f0a320dbf13f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348301 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{\\sqrt{x}-y}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(9,3),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}}{2\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{0}}{6}\\,\\text{d}x-{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x-1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(9+Δx,3+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈\\frac{1}{6}\\,Δx-1\\,Δy\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n{e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2}=f(10,3.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(10,3.2).$\n\nMoving from $(9,3)$ to $(10,3.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =10-9=1, \\\\ Δy & =3.2-3=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈0.167-0.2 \\\\  & ≈-0.033.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $0.033.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10,3.2) & ≈f(9,3)-0.033 \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{10}-3.2} & ≈{e}^{\\sqrt{9}-3}-0.033 \\\\  & ≈1-0.033 \\\\  & ≈0.967.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348306 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186347810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348306 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348306 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348306 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate $(1.06)^{4.1}$ using a differential $\\text{d}f$ of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{y}$ at the point $(1,4).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348306 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6749d91f-49a8-5f4a-a9d1-89ed10459b4a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186348306 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{∂f}{∂x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{∂f}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =y{x}^{y-1}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $(1,4),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =4\\cdot {1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{1}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{d}x+0\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $(1+Δx,4+Δy),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\nΔf≈4\\,Δx+0\\,Δy.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n(1.06)^{4.1}=f(1.06,4.1),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $(1.06,4.1).$\n\nMoving from $(1,4)$ to $(1.06,4.1),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δx & =1.06-1=0.06, \\\\ Δy & =4.1-4=0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δf & ≈4\\cdot (0.06)+0\\cdot (0.1) \\\\  & ≈0.24+0 \\\\  & ≈0.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ increases by about $0.24.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.06,4.1) & ≈f(1,4)+0.24 \\\\ (1.06)^{4.1} & ≈{1}^{4}+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1+0.24 \\\\  & ≈1.24\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352147 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186352147 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 175921